In article <C23Byv.7zx@athena.cs.uga.edu> brown@pollux (Daniel Brown) writes:
>In article <HUTTAR.93Feb6142721@hp750.itg.ti.com> huttar@hp750.itg.ti.com (Lars Huttar) writes:
>>The confirm option, I always thought, was part of the interface to the
>>game, and not the game world itself, and thus shouldn't be affected by
>>conditions in the game... unless those conditions interfere with the
>>perception necessary for what confirm does to work.  E.g. hallucination
>>and stunning *do* interfere with your ability to tell whether the direction
>>you're about to move contains a peaceful monster, so it is fitting that
>>confirm doesn't work on them.  But when you're wielding Stormbringer,
>>you can still perceive the monster's attitude, so losing confirm doesn't
>>make sense.
>
>Well said!  This change is no fun at all.  I hope it is eliminated from the
>next version.

	Actually, this change is much better.  I like it this way, if you are
going to make the sword Stormbringer, then it should act as the original
Stormbringer should.
	The fact is that Stormbringer is a chaotic weapon that is almost
ALWAYS hungry for souls.  The confirm option is there so that you can say,
"no, I don't want to attack that monster."  But when you are wielding
Stormbringer, you have NO CONTROL OVER IT.  That is why there is no
confirmation.  It's all well and good to tell yourself that I should not be
attacking this person, but it's not you who is attacking, it is the sword.
It makes use of your body to feed its hunger.
	What should be done is adding an allowance that either:
	Stormbringer has a "nutritional" variable so that after a time it
will be "sated" and it will function as a slightly better than normal runed
broadsword, but will not suck life.
	Or a character can "fight" the hunger of Stormbringer, much as Elric
did in the original stories.  I suppose it should be a matter of intelligence
and wisdom.

